{
  "posts": [
    {
      "url": "https://x.com/mctursh/status/2114788678782365700",
      "author": "mctursh",
      "text": "Built a tiny async runtime in Rust from scratch to understand what Tokio actually does. Biggest lesson: a future never waits. It tries, leaves its waker, and returns Pending. The kernel watches, the executor sleeps. It spawns, blocks, runs timers, handles cancellation, uses mio for sockets, and runs a TCP echo server on one thread.",
      "date": "2026-10-26T18:38:06Z"
    },
    {
      "url": "https://x.com/zeenix/status/2116483850071048444",
      "author": "zeenix",
      "text": "zruntime 0.2.2 is out! The simple, single-threaded async runtime for Rust now: • watches sockets edge-triggered on epoll/kqueue • makes 20% fewer syscalls when busy • polls block_on's future only when woken https://github.com/z-galaxy/zruntime/releases/tag/zruntime-0.2.2 #rustlang",
      "date": "2026-10-31T10:54:06Z"
    },
    {
      "url": "https://x.com/alexelcu/status/2115943331737862586",
      "author": "alexelcu",
      "text": "TIL that AsyncDrop in #Rust isn't a thing. You can't do RAII with a resource that has an asynchronous destructor (think waiting on transaction commit before continuing), unless you use tokio::task::block_in_place, which requires multi-threading (since it blocks current thread).",
      "date": "2026-10-29T23:06:17Z"
    },
    {
      "url": "https://x.com/codetalkio/status/2115114462801707035",
      "author": "codetalkio",
      "text": "got my boy Claude to go ham on the rust graphql library we were using (async-graphql) because the compile times were insane not only did it reduce compile times of cold builds by ~40% and rebuilds by ~80% but also reduced allocations by ~60% zero runtime regressions 👌",
      "date": "2026-10-27T16:12:39Z"
    },
    {
      "url": "https://x.com/octavianslash/status/2115867121611845704",
      "author": "octavianslash",
      "text": "Why is every async rust library optimised for tokio and send + sync? I don’t want to fork every single library in the async rust ecosystem just to make it work my thread local runtime. There has to be a way to make this work",
      "date": "2026-10-29T18:03:27Z"
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The posts explore building and optimizing custom async runtimes in Rust, including lessons from implementing one from scratch and updates to single-threaded options like zruntime. They also highlight challenges such as the lack of AsyncDrop for RAII, compile-time optimizations in async libraries, and the dominance of Tokio in the ecosystem limiting alternatives."
}
